        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "where  $ \\mathbb{E}[t^2u] = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{k=1}^n t_k^2 u_k $ is the empirical coskewness of the score pair. The auxiliary response weight  $ \\boldsymbol{v} $ is part of the maximization but only  $ \\boldsymbol{w}_i $ is retained – exactly as the covariance criterion (2a) implicitly maximizes over a response direction, which in that case can be eliminated analytically. The  $ j $th  $ \\mathbf{Y} $-side weight  $ \\boldsymbol{v}_j $ symmetrically maximizes  $ \\mathbb{E}[t^2] $ with  $ t = \\mathbf{X}\\boldsymbol{w} $ and  $ u = \\mathbf{Y}_{j-1}\\boldsymbol{v} $. Scores, loadings and the per-block rank-one deflations are exactly those of Section 2, so the two loops may again run to different ranks  $ h $ and  $ g $, and the resulting  $ (\\mathbf{W}, \\mathbf{V}) $ recombine into regression coefficients through (3) unchanged: the coskewness criterion is a drop-in replacement for the covariance criterion inside the twoblock estimator. Because coskewness mixes third-order moments across variables, it is not scale invariant; one has to take care that both blocks are always autoscaled prior to calculating results, so that the objective becomes a moment of standardized scores.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                297,
                1083,
                615
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    136.0,
                    297.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    297.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    615.0
                ],
                [
                    136.0,
                    615.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Third moments are, however, estimated with more sampling variability than second moments, and on data that are close to a single linear law the covariance objective remains preferable. A combined objective interpolates between the two within each loop,",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                617,
                1084,
                705
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
